"Crystal (§{#}): an ion in a salt crystal feels the Coulomb pull of every other ion, which adds up to −α ke²/r per ion pair, where r is the nearest neighbor distance and α the Madelung constant of the structure. A short range repulsion A/rⁿ stops the ions merging; at the equilibrium spacing r₀ the energy is U = −α ke²/r₀ ({#} − {#}/n). The demo gets α by Ewald's method ({#} for NaCl, {#} for CsCl) and shows two ways of adding the ions up: shell by shell, which never settles, and Evjen's neutral cubes, which settle quickly for the NaCl structure. r₀ is from measured lattice constants at room temperature (Kittel, Table {#}; Wyckoff for the cesium salts); n is the average of Pauling's values for the two ions' noble gas cores; lattice energies are the book's Table {#}. The model leaves out the van der Waals attraction and the zero point energy, which is why it falls a few percent short of the table. Ions are drawn at {#} of their ionic radii (Shannon {#}) so the lattice shows through.",
"Bands (§{#}): a chain of N model atoms, each with one s level at −{#} eV and three p levels at −{#} eV (a made up atom, not a real element). Each orbital overlaps only the same orbital on its nearest neighbors, with an overlap energy t that falls off exponentially as the spacing grows (the tight binding model, Ashcroft and Mermin ch. {#}); s and p do not mix. Then each atomic level becomes exactly N levels, E = E_atom − 2t cos(jπ/(N + {#})), which fill a band of width 4t when N is large. Each level holds {#} electrons (spin), and the two p orbitals across the chain give two levels at each energy. Electrons fill the levels from the bottom (T = {#}). A partly filled band makes a metal; a full band with a gap above it makes an insulator or, if the gap is small, a semiconductor. (When the atoms are far apart the band is so narrow that this simple rule no longer applies: electrons that repel each other then stay on their own atoms. The model leaves that out.) A real solid is three dimensional, so a real p band holds 6N electrons as the book says; the chain shows the same counting. Real gaps are the book's Table {#}.",
"Periodic potential (beyond §{#}): the Kronig–Penney model (Kronig and Penney {#}; Kittel, Introduction to Solid State Physics, ch. {#}) replaces the ions by a row of square wells of width w at V = {#}, separated by barriers of height V₀ and width b, period a = w + b. The Schrödinger equation is solved exactly in each flat piece, and Bloch's theorem, ψ(x + a) = e^(ika) ψ(x), leaves the condition cos ka = f(E), where f comes from matching ψ and ψ′ across one period. Energies with |f(E)| > {#} have no wave that runs through the crystal: they are the gaps. The picture shows the real part of the Bloch state (or |ψ|²) of the chosen band and k, drawn on its own energy line with an arbitrary amplitude. The free electron curve ħ²k²/2m is drawn in the extended zone for comparison; with V₀ = {#} the bands join into it. A one dimensional model with square barriers, not a real crystal: the numbers show how bands and gaps arise, not the bands of a particular solid.",
"Doping (§{#}): the electron density n = N_c e^(−(E_c − E_F)/kT) and hole density p = N_v e^(−(E_F − E_v)/kT), with N_c and N_v the effective densities of states (Sze and Ng, Appendix G) growing as T^({#}/{#}). The Fermi level is found from charge neutrality, n + N_A⁻ = p + N_D⁺, with the donors and acceptors only partly ionized (degeneracies {#} and {#}). In a pure sample n = p = n_i = √(N_c N_v) e^(−E_g/2kT). Approximations: the gap is held at its {#} K value ({#} eV for silicon; the book's Table {#} gives {#} eV); Boltzmann statistics, good below about {#}¹⁸ cm⁻³; the impurity levels are those of silicon (Sze and Ng Fig. {#}), so only silicon can be doped here. The Hall readings are the model's n and p, without noise. Simulated data.",
"Junction (§{#}): an ideal diode passes I = I₀(e^(eV/kT) − {#}) (Eq. {#}): forward bias lowers the barrier and the current grows exponentially; reverse bias leaves only −I₀. I₀ scales as n_i², so as T³ e^(−E_g/kT), from the value you set at {#} K; the slower changes of mobility and diffusion length are left out, and so are the series resistance that limits a real diode's current at high bias and the breakdown at large reverse bias. The depletion region and band diagram use an abrupt silicon junction in the depletion approximation (Sze and Ng §{#}), with V_bi = (kT/e) ln(N_A N_D/n_i²); it fails as V approaches V_bi. LEDs: the photon energy is taken as the band gap, E ≈ E_g, so λ = hc/E_g (gaps from the book's Table {#}, GaN from Vurgaftman and coworkers {#}); a real LED's peak sits within a few kT of this. GaP has an indirect gap and is a weak emitter on its own. Simulated data.",
